# The counting program allocator compares its byte counts (live bytes against
# the cap, freed bytes against live, the high-water mark) as unsigned words by
# flipping the sign bit with `bit_xor`. Those sites were spelled `^`, which Jet
# reads as power, so this pins their meaning: a list past the 64 MB cap is
# refused without aborting, and freed bytes come back to the cap, on every
# tier. The first list ends with its match arm: `held = [Int]{}` would empty a
# sole owner in place and keep its buffer, so it gives no bytes back.
fixture: CountingProgramAllocatorRefusesPastItsCapAndTakesBackFreedBytes.fixture
env: NO_COLOR=1
env: JET_STORE_DIR={work}/cache
env: JETPACK_ROOT={work}/jetpack
step: jet check main.jet
exit: 0
step: jet run -O0 main.jet
exit: 0
stdout: = "a 40 MB list fits under the cap\na second 40 MB list beside the first is over the cap\nthe first is still held, with 0 items\nafter the first is freed, a 40 MB list fits again\n40 MB lists freed one after another: 20 of 20 fit\n"
step: jet run -O1 main.jet
exit: 0
stdout: = "a 40 MB list fits under the cap\na second 40 MB list beside the first is over the cap\nthe first is still held, with 0 items\nafter the first is freed, a 40 MB list fits again\n40 MB lists freed one after another: 20 of 20 fit\n"
step: jet run -O2 main.jet
exit: 0
stdout: = "a 40 MB list fits under the cap\na second 40 MB list beside the first is over the cap\nthe first is still held, with 0 items\nafter the first is freed, a 40 MB list fits again\n40 MB lists freed one after another: 20 of 20 fit\n"
